# $project_name

A Contexture MCP server. It declares one role, one skill, one tool, and one
resource, and serves them to any agent runtime that speaks MCP.

## Run it

```bash
uv sync
uv run contexture serve      # serves MCP over stdio; blocks
```

## See what it would serve

```bash
uv run contexture list
```

## Connect a host

The same command for each — the host launches this server itself.

```bash
# Claude Code writes to local scope unless a scope is named.
claude mcp add --scope project $project_name -- uv run contexture serve
codex  mcp add                 $project_name -- uv run contexture serve
```

## Where to write your own

There are three kinds of node and no fourth. A **Role** is a boundary, a
**Skill** is a procedure the model performs, a **Tool** is work the framework
runs. Content is a Tool as well — read-only, taking no arguments — so the file
it sits in is organisation and not a fourth kind.

```text
$project_name/
├── pyproject.toml      the one hand-written list: which roots, and what is
│                       published. It names the doors, never the rooms.
├── assistant/          one role and what it owns — you *subclass* these
│   ├── role.py         the responsibility boundary            Role
│   ├── skills.py       procedures, disclosed when asked for   Skill
│   ├── tools.py        capabilities, as typed Python methods  Tool
│   └── documents.py    content — a read-only Tool, no arguments
└── publish.py          the ways in for a person or a host — you *construct*
                        these: Resource(opens=…) and Prompt(opens=…) name a
                        node the role tree already holds
```

Each root role is a top-level package here, the way a Django app is. This
directory goes on the end of `sys.path` when the server starts, so a module
name it shares with something already installed resolves to the installed one
— `contexture serve` says so rather than serving somebody else's module.

Add a second role by copying `assistant/` to a sibling directory, then either
list it in `roots` or declare it as a child of an existing role.
